# Byte fidelity is load-bearing: container scripts and viewer assets are
# byte-identical copies of skill originals, verified by a copy_fidelity
# check that compares CONTENT. Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true at
# the system level; a checkout that silently rewrote every line ending
# would break that check everywhere at once and look like tampering.
* -text

# The memory logs are append-only. Two branches that both appended have no
# conflict to resolve — they have two sets of facts, and the merge is their
# union in clock order. Git's default text driver cannot know that: it sees
# two additions at the same end of a file and writes a conflict marker INTO
# a log every other verb parses as JSON.
#
# `orchmem` is defined per clone (it names a local interpreter and an
# absolute path), so a fresh clone must run `container_repo.py
# install-hooks` — the same requirement core.hooksPath already carries.
# Without it git falls back to the text driver and conflicts loudly, which
# is the failure to prefer.
memory/graph/*.jsonl merge=orchmem
memory/insight/*.jsonl merge=orchmem
memory/**/graph/*.jsonl merge=orchmem
memory/**/insight/*.jsonl merge=orchmem
